cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this framework, benchmark, or results in your research, please cite it as below."
title: "When Sparse Features Become Mechanistic: Representation Geometry and Causal Interpretability in Physics-Informed Neural Networks"
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Khan
    given-names: Shamique
repository-code: "https://github.com/shamiquekhan/PINN-Mechanistic-Interpretability"
url: "https://github.com/shamiquekhan/PINN-Mechanistic-Interpretability"
abstract: >
  A preregistered research framework answering WHEN sparse-feature
  mechanistic interpretability works on physics-informed neural networks
  (PINNs): the answer is a measurable function of representational
  geometry, with the regime boundary running INSIDE the PINN family.
  In conventional low-rank tanh PINNs (covariance participation ratio
  1.14-2.51; depth lowers it further), SAE and PCA features alike carry
  no effect-magnitude causal information under matched-deletion controls
  (0/8 survivors on either basis; causal-abstraction interchange null).
  A Fourier-feature PINN on the same task crosses into a higher
  covariance-rank regime (PR 4.0-5.6, rho 0.075 on 1D Poisson; PR ~15,
  rho 0.25 on Burgers) where the same TopK SAE beats k-matched PCA by
  25-56x in reconstruction (3/3 seeds, stable across 3x3 SAE seeds) —
  yet the causal battery remains null there too: superposition is
  necessary but not sufficient. A preregistered head-to-head with the
  concurrent PhysSAE protocol on matched-architecture checkpoints shows
  their alignment and spatial-concentration evidence is achieved
  equally by random bases. The chain rank -> compression advantage ->
  causal specificity: the first two arrows measured, the third
  consistently absent. Includes an 8-family PDE suite, TopK and
  ReLU+L1 SAEs with mandatory PCA/ICA/random controls, planted-feature
  positive controls gating every battery, a rollback-capable
  controller (H17: robustness, not per-failure SOTA), leakage-audited
  monitors with an honest trajectory floor (H19), SOTA optimization
  baselines, and a fully preregistered H1-H19 + R1-R5 pipeline with
  machine-checked artifact grounding.
keywords:
  - physics-informed neural networks
  - mechanistic interpretability
  - sparse autoencoders
  - superposition
  - representation geometry
  - causal interventions
  - scientific machine learning
  - negative results
  - reproducibility
  - preregistration
